              "ev-721d826b4efe-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Petitioners argue that bankruptcy priorities must be expressly granted by statute; the default rule is equal priority and distribution."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioners",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "721d826b4efee1f19133afe027215afc2752f491dd1e23063b728e2348fc7a21",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-721d826b4efe-0000-0005"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Petitioners contend that Zurich must show its workers\u2019 compensation insurance policy receivables fall within the statutory phrase of \u00a7\u202f507(a)(4), i.e., contributions to an employee benefit plan arising from services rendered within 180 days."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Strain",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "721d826b4efee1f19133afe027215afc2752f491dd1e23063b728e2348fc7a21",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-721d826b4efe-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 24,
              "start": 24
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Strain, petitioners assert that legislative history shows no intent to incorporate the ERISA definition into the bankruptcy statute, and that doing so would be improper."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioners (counsel MR. VERRILLI)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "721d826b4efee1f19133afe027215afc2752f491dd1e23063b728e2348fc7a21",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-721d826b4efe-0002-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 38,
              "start": 38
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioners (counsel MR. VERRILLI), respondents argue that the statute expressly prohibits subrogation and therefore does not allow a secondary insurer such as a bank with letters of credit to obtain priority."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Respondents (counsel MR. STRAIN)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "721d826b4efee1f19133afe027215afc2752f491dd1e23063b728e2348fc7a21",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-721d826b4efe-0002-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 52,
              "start": 52
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Respondents (counsel MR. STRAIN), respondents maintain that incorporating the ERISA definition into the bankruptcy statute is improper, citing United States v. Reorganized CF&I."
      }
    ],
